#!/usr/bin/env bash
# scripts/git-hooks/pre-rebase — agent worktree-drift guard for `git rebase`
# (ADR-0684, complements the pre-commit-time guard in ADR-0332).
#
# Why this hook exists
# --------------------
# ADR-0332 added a pre-commit-level guard
# (scripts/ci/check-agent-worktree-drift.sh) that refuses commits
# coming from the *main* checkout while one or more agent worktrees
# under .claude/worktrees/agent-* are active. That guard works only at
# commit time. In a 2026-05-22 incident a background train-shepherd
# agent drifted into the main checkout and ran `git rebase
# origin/master` before any commit attempt — by the time pre-commit
# would have fired, the working tree was already a 200-file partial
# rebase state mixed across two branches. The user's open Zed buffer
# silently picked up the regressed file content.
#
# This hook closes that earlier window. `git rebase` invokes
# .git/hooks/pre-rebase with two arguments (upstream and branch); a
# non-zero exit aborts the rebase before any tree mutation. Same
# allow/refuse logic as the pre-commit guard.
#
# Behaviour
# ---------
#   - Allow: the rebase is being issued from inside an agent worktree
#     (`$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)` matches `*/.claude/worktrees/agent-*`).
#   - Allow: there are no agent worktrees on this clone (the typical
#     contributor path).
#   - Refuse: at least one agent worktree under
#     `<toplevel>/.claude/worktrees/agent-*` exists AND the rebase is
#     being issued from the main checkout. The user can bypass with
#     `git rebase --no-verify` (standard escape hatch — pass it on the
#     `git rebase` command line for legitimate main-checkout rebases
#     while an agent runs).
#
# Bypass: prepend `--no-verify` to the rebase command.
#
# References:
#   - docs/adr/0684-pre-rebase-worktree-drift-guard.md
#   - docs/adr/0332-agent-worktree-drift-hard-guard.md
#   - docs/development/agent-worktree-discipline.md
#   - scripts/ci/check-agent-worktree-drift.sh (sibling pre-commit guard)

set -eu

toplevel="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || true)"
if [ -z "$toplevel" ]; then
  exit 0
fi

# Allow: rebase from inside an agent worktree (intended path).
case "$toplevel" in
  */.claude/worktrees/agent-*)
    exit 0
    ;;
esac

agent_dir="$toplevel/.claude/worktrees"
if [ ! -d "$agent_dir" ]; then
  exit 0
fi

shopt -s nullglob 2>/dev/null || true
agent_worktrees=("$agent_dir"/agent-*)
if [ "${#agent_worktrees[@]}" -eq 0 ]; then
  exit 0
fi

# At least one agent worktree present. Refuse the rebase.
cat >&2 <<EOF
ERROR: agent-worktree-drift guard (ADR-0684) refused this rebase.

You are about to rebase the MAIN checkout while one or more isolated
agent worktrees are active:

EOF
max_print=8
count=0
for wt in "${agent_worktrees[@]}"; do
  if [ "$count" -ge "$max_print" ]; then
    printf '  ... (%d more)\n' \
      "$((${#agent_worktrees[@]} - max_print))" >&2
    break
  fi
  printf '  %s\n' "$wt" >&2
  count=$((count + 1))
done
cat >&2 <<EOF

A rebase in the main tree while agents own worktrees here is the
exact drift pattern from the 2026-05-22 incident — the rebase
clobbered the user's working state across 200+ files before any
commit was attempted, well before the pre-commit guard could fire.

Fixes (pick one):

  1. If you are an agent: \`cd\` into your assigned worktree
     (\`\$AGENT_WT\`) and re-run the rebase from there. Use
     \`git -C "\$AGENT_WT" rebase ...\` from now on.

  2. If you are the human user rebasing legitimate main-checkout
     work while an agent is running: pass --no-verify to git-rebase
     to bypass this hook.

  3. If the listed worktree is stale, remove it with
     \`git worktree remove <path>\` and retry the rebase.

See docs/development/agent-worktree-discipline.md for the full
process-side + host-side discipline.
EOF
exit 1
